Since we have $\cos(2\theta)$ in our expression, let's rewrite it using one of its double angle identities.

Since the expression in the numerator includes $\sin(\theta)$, the most fitting identity is $\cos(2\theta)=1-2\sin^2(\theta)$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{1+\sqrt 2\sin(\theta)}{\cos(2\theta)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1+\sqrt 2\sin(\theta)}{1-2\sin^2(\theta)}\qquad\gray{\cos(2\theta)\text{ identity}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1+\sqrt 2\sin(\theta)}{(1+\sqrt 2\sin(\theta))(1-\sqrt 2\sin(\theta))}\qquad\gray{\text{Diff. of squares}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{1+\sqrt 2\sin(\theta)}}{\cancel{(1+\sqrt 2\sin(\theta))}(1-\sqrt 2\sin(\theta))}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{1-\sqrt 2\sin(\theta)}\text{, for }\theta\neq \{...,-\dfrac{3}{4}\pi, -\dfrac{1}{4}\pi, \dfrac{5}{4}\pi, \dfrac{7}{4}\pi, ...\}
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $\theta $-values (in their domains) except for $-\dfrac{1}{4}\pi+2\pi k$ or $-\dfrac{3}{4}\pi+2\pi k$ for any integer $k$, and specifically $-\dfrac{\pi}{4}$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
